se,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer correctly separates the three risks. Borrower default means securities may not be returned. Collateral shortfall means collateral may fall below replacement cost. Cash reinvestment means investments of cash collateral can lose value or become illiquid. It also correctly says safeguards mitigate but do not eliminate loss, and it keeps UCITS rules distinct from the US iShares example, including the UCITS ability to recall loans or terminate lending at any time. There are substantive omissions, though. (1) For the US/iShares example, it never states the key control that collateral is maintained at least equal to the current market value of loaned securities. Its controls (haircuts, overcollateralization, borrower limits) are generic and not tied to the SAI. (2) For UCITS, it covers non-cash collateral valuation, haircuts and diversification, but omits that non-cash collateral cannot be reinvested. It also omits that UCITS cash-collateral reinvestment is restricted to limited eligible uses and must be diversified, and it does not mention the liquidity and credit-quality criteria. Instead, its cash-reinvestment controls are generic rather than UCITS-specific. The central structure is useful, but key requested regime-specific facts are missing.\"}","ttft_ms":2765,"type":"result","duration_ms":6531,"uuid":"1e6e8dd3-94a4-4cba-b4ea-164f792213bf","ttft_stream_ms":492,"time_to_request_ms":54,"first_content_frame_ms":492,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
